// A monitor's EDID read by the driver itself over the DDC line of an HDMI
// or DVI connector (and of a DisplayPort connector that sends TMDS through
// a passive adapter), by the card's own I2C engine, on a Polaris card (DCE
// 11.2). So far the only EDID the driver had was the one the EFI loader
// got from the firmware: one monitor's, read once, before brixOS started.
// With this the driver asks the monitor that is on a connector now: at
// boot, and when something is plugged in while brixOS runs (gpu_plug.mlx
// says when; gpu.mlx lends the card). Built for the host too
// (tests/484_gpu_ddc_edid_runtime.mlx, against a card that is a table
// with an EDID memory behind it): the card is reached through
// gpu_display's `Card` (a register read, the clock) and `Hands` here (a
// register write).
//
// Everything is Linux 6.6's display core as it runs on this card, read
// function by function (drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/dc): dce/dce_i2c.c
// and dce_i2c_hw.c (the engine), gpio/hw_ddc.c, hw_gpio.c and
// gpio_service.c (the pins), dce112/dce112_resource.c (which engine, the
// speed), bios/bios_parser.c (the line from the ROM), and above them
// drivers/gpu/drm/drm_edid.c (which bytes are asked for, how often) by
// way of amdgpu_dm.c's amdgpu_dm_i2c_xfer. Register numbers and fields
// are dce_11_2_d.h's and dce_11_2_sh_mask.h's, held against them by
// tools/check_gpu_registers.py; none is from memory.
//
// WHICH LINE. A connector's I2C record in the ROM's object table names an
// entry of the ROM's table of DDC lines (GPIO_I2C_Info) by number, and
// says whether the card's own I2C engine may be used on it (bit 7:
// bios_parser.c's get_gpio_i2c_info). The entry's register for the clock
// pin is one of DC_GPIO_DDC1_A to DC_GPIO_DDC6_A, and which one says the
// line: 0 to 5 (gpio/dce110/hw_translate_dce110.c's offset_to_id). The
// engine has a SPEED and a SETUP word for each line and is told the line
// in DC_I2C_DDC_SELECT (dce112_i2c_hw_create: the engine's number is the
// line's). `wireOf` works that out. The log names a line by the ROM's
// number for it (the record's, as the connector's own line of the video
// BIOS says it) and by the card's name for it, DDC1 to DDC6: "DDC line
// 0, the card's DDC3" is line 2 here on a ROM whose entry 0 is DDC3.
//
// A TRANSFER (`Transfer`: begin, the engine's run waited for, the bytes
// taken, the engine let go), with every word, in order:
//   is the engine free: the line's HW_STATUS not "used by the hardware",
//   DC_I2C_ARBITRATION's DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS not "the
//   microcontroller's alone" (is_engine_available). Linux then drives the
//   two pins itself (dce_i2c_sw.c); that is not built: the transfer is
//   refused, and said.
//   The pins (acquire_i2c_hw_engine -> dal_ddc_open in hardware mode,
//   hw_ddc.c's set_config for I2C): DC_GPIO_DDCn_MASK with the data and
//   the clock pin's mask bits 0 (the pins are the engine's) and both
//   pull-down enables set; then, when the pad is the AUX channel's
//   (AUX_PADn_MODE), 2 ms waited if a pin's output enable was set, and the
//   bit cleared.
//   The engine (setup_engine, set_speed): DC_I2C_ARBITRATION's
//   DC_I2C_SW_USE_I2C_REG_REQ set; the line's SPEED: the prescale for
//   100 kHz out of MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV (read only) or the ROM's
//   crystal, threshold 2, start and stop timing 2; DC_I2C_CONTROL: the
//   status reset, the line selected; the line's SETUP: time limit 255,
//   enabled; DC_I2C_ARBITRATION's DC_I2C_NO_QUEUED_SW_GO cleared.
//   Each message (process_transaction): DC_I2C_TRANSACTIONk (start, stop
//   on a missing acknowledge, read or write, how many bytes, a stop after
//   the last), its address byte and a write's bytes into DC_I2C_DATA (the
//   first with the buffer's index set to 0).
//   The run (submit_channel_request_hw, execute_transaction):
//   DC_I2C_CONTROL's status reset; the line's SETUP's drive bits and
//   delays 0; DC_I2C_CONTROL: resets off, the count of messages less one;
//   DC_I2C_CONTROL's DC_I2C_GO.
//   The wait (dce_i2c_hw_engine_wait_on_operation_result): DC_I2C_SW_
//   STATUS read every microsecond while it says "used by software", at
//   most 320 us times (2 + 8 for each byte of the last message and its
//   address + 10 for each message before it) times: 0.334 s for an EDID
//   block. Then judged (get_channel_status): stopped on a missing
//   acknowledge, the engine's time limit, aborted, else done.
//   A read's bytes (process_channel_reply): DC_I2C_DATA's index set
//   behind the bytes that were sent, for reading; then DC_I2C_DATA read
//   once a byte. (Reading that word moves the index on: it is read here
//   and nowhere else.)
//   The engine let go (release_engine): DC_I2C_CONTROL's status reset,
//   with a soft reset when the engine still says "used by software";
//   DC_I2C_ARBITRATION: done, the request cleared.
// Where Linux writes one word twice in a row with the same value (the
// request in setup_engine, the speed three times a transfer), it is
// written once here.
//
// WHAT IS WRITTEN, AND PUT BACK. A transfer writes these words of the
// card and no others: DC_GPIO_DDCn_MASK of the line; DC_I2C_ARBITRATION;
// DC_I2C_CONTROL; the line's DC_I2C_DDCn_SPEED and DC_I2C_DDCn_SETUP;
// DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0 to 2, as many as it has messages; DC_I2C_DATA. None
// of them is a controller's, a clock's, an encoder's or a transmitter's:
// a picture that runs is not touched. Each but the last is read before
// the first write and, when the transfer is over, written back as it was
// found where it holds something else (ours: Linux leaves the engine as a
// transfer left it, the pull-downs on and the pad in I2C mode);
// DC_I2C_CONTROL goes back without its DC_I2C_GO, which starts a run.
// What cannot be put back: DC_I2C_DATA (the engine's buffer and the index
// into it; every transfer fills it anew before it runs) and the engine's
// status word (never written: its bits are cleared through
// DC_I2C_CONTROL). The pins' output words (DC_GPIO_DDCn_A and _EN) are
// only read. `restored` says whether every word took its old value again.
//
// AN EDID (`Reader`), as drm_edid.c asks for it: first one byte, to see
// whether anything answers at the EDID's address 0x50 (drm_probe_ddc);
// then the base block; then as many extension blocks as its byte 126
// names, three at most here (512 bytes are kept). A block: its place
// written to 0x50 (0 or 128), 128 bytes read, in one run of the engine;
// for a block beyond the second the segment pointer (the block's number
// halved) is written to 0x30 first in the same run (it falls back to 0 at
// the run's stop). A transfer that fails is made again, five times in all
// (drm_do_probe_ddc_edid); a block that does not hold (the base block's
// header, a block's sum) is read again, four times in all
// (edid_block_read). Ours: the whole read has two seconds and begins no
// transfer after them; an extension block that cannot be read or does not
// hold ends the EDID before it (Linux gives the whole EDID up when one
// cannot be read, drops one that does not hold and mends the count, and
// keeps a CTA extension whose sum is wrong, with a word in its log:
// edid_block_status_valid; here that block is not taken, as edid.mlx
// reads no HDMI block out of an extension that does not add up, so such
// a monitor counts as a DVI one where Linux would send it HDMI); a
// header with a few wrong bytes is not mended; a base block of another
// version than 1 is not refused here (edid.mlx reads what it can of
// it); the HDMI Forum's count of blocks is not followed.
//
// NOTHING HERE WAITS BY ITSELF. `Transfer.step` and `Reader.step` do a
// bounded piece of work and say what they need next: to be called again
// at once, or after a pause while the engine runs or the pad settles
// (`pause`: how many microseconds at least). At boot `read` goes through
// the steps with the pauses Linux makes (a microsecond between two looks
// at the status); while brixOS runs the timer's tick takes a step each
// time (`paced` false: the bound is then by the clock), so the timer's
// interrupt never waits for a monitor. One transfer at a time on the
// whole card: the engine is one for all lines (Linux's
// i2c_hw_buffer_in_use), and the AUX channel of a DisplayPort connector
// uses the same two pins.
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const videorom = @import("./gpu_rom.mlx")

pub const NONE: usize = 0xFFFFFFFF
// The card's DDC lines (dce112_resource.c's num_ddc: six on Polaris 10,
// five on Polaris 11 and 12).
pub const LINES: usize = 6
pub const LINES_SMALL: usize = 5

// The engine: one control, arbitration and status word, four messages'
// words and the data word for all lines; a status, a speed and a setup
// word for each line (the first line's numbers; HW_STATUS a number
// apart, SPEED and SETUP two).
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL: usize = 0x16D4
pub const DC_I2C_ARBITRATION: usize = 0x16D5
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS: usize = 0x16D7
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS: usize = 0x16D8
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED: usize = 0x16DE
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP: usize = 0x16DF
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0: usize = 0x16EA
pub const DC_I2C_DATA: usize = 0x16EE
// What the engine's clock is made from (read only).
pub const MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV: usize = 0x13B
// A line's two pins (the first line's numbers; four apart): which of
// them the hardware has, what they would put out, and whether they do.
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK: usize = 0x4868
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_A: usize = 0x4869
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN: usize = 0x486A

pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_GO: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SOFT_RESET: usize = 0x2
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SEND_RESET: usize = 0x4
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET: usize = 0x8
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_DDC_SELECT: usize = 0x700
pub const DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_TRANSACTION_COUNT: usize = 0x300000
pub const DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS: usize = 0xC
pub const DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_NO_QUEUED_SW_GO: usize = 0x10
pub const DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_SW_USE_I2C_REG_REQ: usize = 0x100000
pub const DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_SW_DONE_USING_I2C_REG: usize = 0x200000
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS: usize = 0x3
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_DONE: usize = 0x4
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_ABORTED: usize = 0x10
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_TIMEOUT: usize = 0x20
pub const DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STOPPED_ON_NACK: usize = 0x100
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS__DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS: usize = 0x3
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_THRESHOLD: usize = 0x3
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_START_STOP_TIMING_CNTL: usize = 0x300
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_PRESCALE: usize = 0xFFFF0000
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_DATA_DRIVE_EN: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_DATA_DRIVE_SEL: usize = 0x2
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_ENABLE: usize = 0x40
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_CLK_DRIVE_EN: usize = 0x80
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_INTRA_BYTE_DELAY: usize = 0xFF00
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_INTRA_TRANSACTION_DELAY: usize = 0xFF0000
pub const DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_TIME_LIMIT: usize = 0xFF000000
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_RW0: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_STOP_ON_NACK0: usize = 0x100
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_START0: usize = 0x1000
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_STOP0: usize = 0x2000
pub const DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_COUNT0: usize = 0x3FF0000
pub const DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA_RW: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA: usize = 0xFF00
pub const DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_INDEX: usize = 0x3FF0000
pub const DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_INDEX_WRITE: usize = 0x80000000
pub const MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV__MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV: usize = 0x7F
pub const MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV__XTAL_REF_DIV: usize = 0x7F00
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_MASK: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_PD_EN: usize = 0x10
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_MASK: usize = 0x100
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_PD_EN: usize = 0x1000
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__AUX_PAD1_MODE: usize = 0x10000
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_EN: usize = 0x1
pub const DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_EN: usize = 0x100

// What the status fields say (dce_i2c_hw.h's dc_i2c_status).
pub const DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_IDLE: usize = 0
pub const DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_SW: usize = 1
pub const DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_HW: usize = 2
pub const DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS_DMCU_ONLY: usize = 2
// Linux's numbers for the engine on this card (dce_i2c_hw.h, dce112_
// resource.c's i2c_speed_in_khz): the buffer's bytes, the setup word's
// time limit, how many clock periods a byte's bit may take, the speed in
// kHz; and what a speed word gets beside the prescale (set_speed).
pub const I2C_HW_BUFFER_SIZE_DCE100: usize = 538
pub const I2C_SETUP_TIME_LIMIT_DCE: usize = 255
pub const TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT_IN_I2C_CLOCKS: usize = 32
pub const SPEED: usize = 100
pub const THRESHOLD: usize = 2
pub const START_STOP_TIMING: usize = 2
// The pad's time to let go before it is switched from AUX to I2C
// (hw_ddc.c: msleep(2)), in microseconds.
pub const PAD_SETTLE: usize = 2000
// drm_edid.c's and drm_edid.h's: the EDID's address and the segment
// pointer's, a block's bytes, how often a transfer and a block are tried.
pub const DDC_ADDR: usize = 0x50
pub const DDC_SEGMENT_ADDR: usize = 0x30
pub const EDID_LENGTH: usize = 128
pub const TRANSFER_TRIES: usize = 5
pub const BLOCK_READS: usize = 4
// Ours: how many blocks are kept, and how long a whole EDID's reading
// may take before no further transfer is begun (nanoseconds).
pub const BLOCKS: usize = 4
pub const ROOM: usize = 512
pub const BUDGET: usize = 2000000000

// The ROM's table of DDC lines (atombios.h's ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO: a
// table's four bytes of head, then lines of 27 bytes, ATOM_GPIO_I2C_
// ASSIGMENT, of which the clock pin's register is read) and its place in
// the ROM's list of data tables.
pub const GPIO_I2C_Info: usize = 10
pub const ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO__asGPIO_Info: usize = 4
pub const ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__usClkA_RegisterIndex: usize = 6
pub const ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__size: usize = 27

// The card as a transfer writes to it: a register by its number.
pub const Hands = struct {
    write: fn(usize, u32) -> void

    pub fn put(self: *const Self, number: usize, value: usize) -> void {
        const write = self.*.write
        write(number, @intCast(u32, value & 0xFFFFFFFF))
    }
}

// Why a connector's DDC line is not known.
pub const Unwired = enum(u8) {
    wired,
    // The connector has no I2C record.
    no_record,
    // The ROM has no table of DDC lines, or one of another revision than
    // Linux's display core reads (content revision 1), or one that does
    // not lie inside the ROM.
    no_table,
    // The record names a line the table does not have.
    beyond_table,
    // The line's clock pin is none of the card's six DDC lines'.
    not_a_line
}

// A connector's DDC line as the ROM gives it: the card's line (0 to 5),
// the number the ROM's record names it by, and whether the record says
// the card's own engine may be used on it.
pub const Wire = struct {
    line: usize
    entry: usize
    hardware: bool
    why: Unwired
}

fn byteAt(bytes: []const u8, at: usize) -> usize { return if at < bytes.length { @intCast(usize, bytes[at]) } else { 0 } }

fn wordAt(bytes: []const u8, at: usize) -> usize { return byteAt(bytes, at) | (byteAt(bytes, at + 1) << 8) }

// The DDC line of a connector (get_gpio_i2c_info, then offset_to_id).
// Linux takes a record's number that is the table's count too, and reads
// behind the table for it; here it has to be below the count.
pub fn wireOf(machine: *const atom.Atom, connector: *const videorom.Connector) -> Wire {
    var wire = Wire.{ .line = NONE, .entry = connector.*.ddc, .hardware = connector.*.ddcHardware, .why = Unwired.no_record }
    if connector.*.ddc == videorom.NONE { return wire }
    const image = machine.*.rom
    const table = machine.dataTable(GPIO_I2C_Info)
    wire.why = Unwired.no_table
    if table == 0 || table + ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO__asGPIO_Info > image.length || byteAt(image, table + 3) != 1 { return wire }
    const length = wordAt(image, table)
    if length < ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO__asGPIO_Info + ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__size || table + length > image.length { return wire }
    const count = (length - ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO__asGPIO_Info) / ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__size
    wire.why = Unwired.beyond_table
    if connector.*.ddc >= count { return wire }
    const register = wordAt(image, table + ATOM_GPIO_I2C_INFO__asGPIO_Info + connector.*.ddc * ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__size + ATOM_GPIO_I2C_ASSIGMENT__usClkA_RegisterIndex)
    wire.why = Unwired.not_a_line
    if register < DC_GPIO_DDC1_A || (register - DC_GPIO_DDC1_A) % 4 != 0 || (register - DC_GPIO_DDC1_A) / 4 >= LINES { return wire }
    wire.line = (register - DC_GPIO_DDC1_A) / 4
    wire.why = Unwired.wired
    return wire
}

// How a transfer ended.
pub const Failure = enum(u8) {
    none,
    // Refused before anything was written: there is no line; the ROM
    // does not say the card's engine may be used on it; the card has
    // fewer lines; the engine's words do not answer; no clock for the
    // engine can be worked out; the line is used by the card's hardware,
    // or the engine's words are the card's microcontroller's alone.
    no_line,
    not_hardware,
    no_such_line,
    no_answer_from_card,
    no_clock,
    taken_by_hardware,
    taken_by_controller,
    // The engine says it is busy before it was started.
    busy,
    // Nobody acknowledged an address or a byte: nothing is there, or it
    // refused.
    not_acknowledged,
    // The engine's own time limit; the engine gave the run up.
    time_limit,
    aborted,
    // The engine still says "used by software" after Linux's bound.
    stays_busy
}

pub fn failureText(failure: Failure) -> []const u8 {
    return match failure {
        .none => "nothing failed",
        .no_line => "the ROM names no DDC line of the card's for the connector",
        .not_hardware => "the ROM does not say the card's I2C engine may be used on the line (Linux then drives the pins itself, which is not built)",
        .no_such_line => "the card has no such DDC line",
        .no_answer_from_card => "the I2C engine's registers do not answer",
        .no_clock => "no clock for the I2C engine can be worked out",
        .taken_by_hardware => "the card's hardware uses the line",
        .taken_by_controller => "the I2C engine is the card's microcontroller's alone",
        .busy => "the I2C engine says it is busy before it was started",
        .not_acknowledged => "nothing acknowledged",
        .time_limit => "the I2C engine ran into its time limit",
        .aborted => "the I2C engine gave the run up",
        .stays_busy => "the I2C engine stayed busy"
    }
}

// One message of a transfer: the address (seven bits), which way, how
// many bytes, and a write's bytes.
pub const Message = struct {
    address: usize
    read: bool
    count: usize
    bytes: [2]u8
}

pub const MESSAGES: usize = 3

// What `step` needs next: to be called again at once; again after
// `pause` microseconds at least; nothing, it is over.
pub const Next = enum(u8) { again, wait, done }

const Phase = enum(u8) { fresh, pad, loading, running, over }

// The words a transfer found, to put them back.
pub const Kept = struct {
    mask: usize
    arbitration: usize
    control: usize
    speed: usize
    setup: usize
    transaction: [MESSAGES]usize
}

// The lowest bit of a field.
fn shiftOf(mask: usize) -> u6 {
    var shift: usize = 0
    while shift < 32 && (mask >> @intCast(u6, shift)) & 1 == 0 { shift += 1 }
    return @intCast(u6, shift)
}

// `word` with the field `mask` holding `value`.
fn with(word: usize, mask: usize, value: usize) -> usize { return (word & ~mask) | ((value << shiftOf(mask)) & mask) }

fn fieldOf(word: usize, mask: usize) -> usize { return (word & mask) >> shiftOf(mask) }

// What a status word says of a run that is over (get_channel_status).
fn judged(status: usize) -> Failure {
    if status & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STOPPED_ON_NACK != 0 { return Failure.not_acknowledged }
    if status & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_TIMEOUT != 0 { return Failure.time_limit }
    if status & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_ABORTED != 0 { return Failure.aborted }
    return Failure.none
}

pub const Transfer = struct {
    // What is asked: the line (0 to 5), how many lines the card has, the
    // ROM's crystal in kHz (the engine's clock when the card's time base
    // word names none), and the messages: writes, then at most one read,
    // which is the last.
    line: usize
    lines: usize
    crystal: usize
    messages: [MESSAGES]Message
    count: usize
    // How it stands: what `step` needs, how it ended, the microseconds to
    // pause when it waits.
    phase: Phase
    failure: Failure
    pause: usize
    // The words found; whether anything was written (then the engine is
    // let go and the words put back whatever happens); whether each word
    // took its old value again, and the first that did not.
    kept: Kept
    touched: bool
    restored: bool
    stuck: usize
    // The prescale worked out, the bytes put into the engine's buffer,
    // the bound of the wait (Linux's count of looks a microsecond apart:
    // so many microseconds), the looks made and the clock when the run
    // was started, when the pad may be switched, the status word as last
    // read, how many writes were made.
    prescale: usize
    used: usize
    bound: usize
    looks: usize
    started: usize
    padUntil: usize
    status: usize
    writes: usize
    // A read's bytes.
    received: [EDID_LENGTH]u8
    got: usize

    // The transfer set up for `count` messages on `line`; nothing of the
    // card is touched yet.
    pub fn begin(self: *Self, line: usize, lines: usize, crystal: usize) -> void {
        self.*.line = line
        self.*.lines = lines
        self.*.crystal = crystal
        self.*.count = 0
        self.*.phase = Phase.fresh
        self.*.failure = Failure.none
        self.*.pause = 0
        self.*.touched = false
        self.*.restored = true
        self.*.stuck = NONE
        self.*.prescale = 0
        self.*.used = 0
        self.*.bound = 0
        self.*.looks = 0
        self.*.started = 0
        self.*.padUntil = 0
        self.*.status = 0
        self.*.writes = 0
        self.*.got = 0
    }

    // A message more: `value` written to `address`, or `count` bytes read
    // from it.
    pub fn write(self: *Self, address: usize, value: usize) -> void {
        if self.*.count == MESSAGES { return }
        self.*.messages[self.*.count] = Message.{ .address = address, .read = false, .count = 1, .bytes = [_]u8{ @intCast(u8, value & 0xFF), 0 } }
        self.*.count += 1
    }

    // (A block's 128 bytes and the few before them are far below what
    // the engine's buffer holds, I2C_HW_BUFFER_SIZE_DCE100, which Linux
    // holds every message against.)
    pub fn read(self: *Self, address: usize, count: usize) -> void {
        if self.*.count == MESSAGES { return }
        self.*.messages[self.*.count] = Message.{ .address = address, .read = true, .count = if count > EDID_LENGTH { EDID_LENGTH } else { count }, .bytes = [_]u8{ 0, 0 } }
        self.*.count += 1
    }

    pub fn over(self: *const Self) -> bool { return self.*.phase == Phase.over }

    pub fn succeeded(self: *const Self) -> bool { return self.*.phase == Phase.over && self.*.failure == Failure.none }

    fn put(self: *Self, hands: *const Hands, number: usize, value: usize) -> void {
        hands.put(number, value)
        self.*.writes += 1
        self.*.touched = true
    }

    // A word read, changed and written (Linux's REG_UPDATE): the bits of
    // `clear` 0, then those of `set` 1.
    fn update(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, number: usize, clear: usize, set: usize) -> void {
        self.put(hands, number, (card.reg(number) & ~clear) | set)
    }

    fn ended(self: *Self, failure: Failure) -> Next {
        self.*.failure = failure
        self.*.phase = Phase.over
        return Next.done
    }

    // Whether the engine may be used: the line is not the hardware's and
    // the engine's words are not the microcontroller's alone
    // (is_engine_available).
    fn available(self: *const Self, card: *const scanout.Card) -> Failure {
        if card.reg(DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS + self.*.line) & DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS__DC_I2C_DDC1_HW_STATUS == DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_HW { return Failure.taken_by_hardware }
        if fieldOf(card.reg(DC_I2C_ARBITRATION), DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS) == DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS_DMCU_ONLY { return Failure.taken_by_controller }
        return Failure.none
    }

    // Whether the engine is busy with something else (is_hw_busy): its
    // status is not idle and it may not be used.
    fn busy(self: *const Self, card: *const scanout.Card) -> bool {
        if card.reg(DC_I2C_SW_STATUS) & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS == DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_IDLE { return false }
        return self.available(card) != Failure.none
    }

    // The engine's prescale for SPEED kHz (set_speed); 0 when none can be
    // worked out or it does not fit its field.
    fn prescaleOf(self: *const Self, card: *const scanout.Card) -> usize {
        const base = card.reg(MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV)
        if base == 0xFFFFFFFF { return 0 }
        var divider = fieldOf(base, MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV__XTAL_REF_DIV)
        if divider == 0 { divider = 2 }
        const microsecond = base & MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV__MICROSECOND_TIME_BASE_DIV
        const clock = if microsecond == 0 { (self.*.crystal >> 1) * 2 } else { microsecond * 1000 }
        const prescale = clock / divider / SPEED
        return if prescale > 0xFFFF { 0 } else { prescale }
    }

    // The next piece of the transfer. `paced`: the caller pauses as
    // `pause` says between two calls (then the run's wait is counted in
    // looks, as Linux counts it); else it comes back when it likes and
    // the waits are held against the clock.
    pub fn step(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, paced: bool) -> Next {
        if self.*.phase == Phase.over { return Next.done }
        const mask = DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK + 4 * self.*.line
        const setup = DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP + 2 * self.*.line
        if self.*.phase == Phase.fresh {
            // Everything that can refuse, before a write.
            if self.*.line == NONE { return self.ended(Failure.no_line) }
            if self.*.line >= self.*.lines || self.*.line >= LINES { return self.ended(Failure.no_such_line) }
            if self.*.count == 0 { return self.ended(Failure.none) }
            self.*.kept.mask = card.reg(mask)
            self.*.kept.arbitration = card.reg(DC_I2C_ARBITRATION)
            self.*.kept.control = card.reg(DC_I2C_CONTROL)
            self.*.kept.speed = card.reg(DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED + 2 * self.*.line)
            self.*.kept.setup = card.reg(setup)
            var k: usize = 0
            while k < self.*.count {
                self.*.kept.transaction[k] = card.reg(DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0 + k)
                k += 1
            }
            if self.*.kept.mask == 0xFFFFFFFF || self.*.kept.control == 0xFFFFFFFF || self.*.kept.arbitration == 0xFFFFFFFF { return self.ended(Failure.no_answer_from_card) }
            const free = self.available(card)
            if free != Failure.none { return self.ended(free) }
            self.*.prescale = self.prescaleOf(card)
            if self.*.prescale == 0 { return self.ended(Failure.no_clock) }
            // The pins: the engine's, pulled down inside; the pad in I2C
            // mode, after its pins had time to let go.
            var pins = self.*.kept.mask & ~(DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_MASK | DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_MASK)
            pins |= DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_PD_EN | DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_PD_EN
            if pins != self.*.kept.mask { self.put(hands, mask, pins) }
            self.*.phase = Phase.loading
            if pins & DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__AUX_PAD1_MODE != 0 {
                self.*.phase = Phase.pad
                const driven = card.reg(DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN + 4 * self.*.line) & (DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN__DC_GPIO_DDC1DATA_EN | DC_GPIO_DDC1_EN__DC_GPIO_DDC1CLK_EN) != 0
                if driven {
                    self.*.padUntil = card.time() + PAD_SETTLE * 1000
                    self.*.pause = PAD_SETTLE
                    return Next.wait
                }
                self.*.padUntil = 0
            }
            return Next.again
        }
        if self.*.phase == Phase.pad {
            if !paced && self.*.padUntil != 0 && card.time() < self.*.padUntil {
                self.*.pause = PAD_SETTLE
                return Next.wait
            }
            self.update(card, hands, mask, DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK__AUX_PAD1_MODE, 0)
            self.*.phase = Phase.loading
            return Next.again
        }
        if self.*.phase == Phase.loading {
            // The engine: asked for, its speed, the line, its time limit.
            self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION, 0, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_SW_USE_I2C_REG_REQ)
            const speed = card.reg(DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED + 2 * self.*.line)
            self.put(hands, DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED + 2 * self.*.line, with(with(with(speed, DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_PRESCALE, self.*.prescale), DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_THRESHOLD, THRESHOLD), DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED__DC_I2C_DDC1_START_STOP_TIMING_CNTL, START_STOP_TIMING))
            const control = card.reg(DC_I2C_CONTROL) & ~(DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_GO | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SOFT_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SEND_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_TRANSACTION_COUNT)
            self.put(hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, with(control | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET, DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_DDC_SELECT, self.*.line))
            self.put(hands, setup, with(card.reg(setup), DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_TIME_LIMIT, I2C_SETUP_TIME_LIMIT_DCE) | DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_ENABLE)
            self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_NO_QUEUED_SW_GO, 0)
            // The messages into the engine's words and its buffer.
            var k: usize = 0
            while k < self.*.count {
                const message = &self.*.messages[k]
                const last = k + 1 == self.*.count
                if self.busy(card) { return self.letGo(card, hands, Failure.busy) }
                var word = card.reg(DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0 + k) & ~(DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_RW0 | DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_STOP0)
                word |= DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_STOP_ON_NACK0 | DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_START0
                if message.*.read { word |= DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_RW0 }
                if last { word |= DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_STOP0 }
                self.put(hands, DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0 + k, with(word, DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0__DC_I2C_COUNT0, message.*.count))
                const address = ((message.*.address << 1) | (if message.*.read { 1 } else { 0 })) & 0xFF
                self.put(hands, DC_I2C_DATA, (address << shiftOf(DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA)) | (if k == 0 { DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_INDEX_WRITE } else { 0 }))
                self.*.used += 1
                if !message.*.read {
                    var b: usize = 0
                    while b < message.*.count {
                        self.put(hands, DC_I2C_DATA, @intCast(usize, message.*.bytes[b]) << shiftOf(DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA))
                        self.*.used += 1
                        b += 1
                    }
                }
                if !last {
                    // (Linux looks at the status after a message that
                    // does not end the run and finds the engine idle; one
                    // that says it runs already, or failed, is not
                    // started.)
                    const now = card.reg(DC_I2C_SW_STATUS)
                    self.*.status = now
                    if now & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS == DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_SW { return self.letGo(card, hands, Failure.busy) }
                    if judged(now) != Failure.none { return self.letGo(card, hands, judged(now)) }
                } else {
                    // The bound of the wait, as Linux works it out before
                    // the last message goes in (get_transaction_timeout_
                    // hw): the earlier messages count a byte each.
                    self.*.bound = 1000 * TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT_IN_I2C_CLOCKS / SPEED * (1 + ((message.*.count + 1) << 3) + 1 + (k << 3) + (k << 1))
                }
                k += 1
            }
            // The run.
            if self.busy(card) { return self.letGo(card, hands, Failure.busy) }
            self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, 0, DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET)
            self.update(card, hands, setup, DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_DATA_DRIVE_EN | DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_CLK_DRIVE_EN | DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_DATA_DRIVE_SEL | DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_INTRA_TRANSACTION_DELAY | DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP__DC_I2C_DDC1_INTRA_BYTE_DELAY, 0)
            const ready = card.reg(DC_I2C_CONTROL) & ~(DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SOFT_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SEND_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_GO)
            self.put(hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, with(ready, DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_TRANSACTION_COUNT, self.*.count - 1))
            self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, 0, DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_GO)
            self.*.started = card.time()
            self.*.looks = 0
            self.*.pause = 1
            self.*.phase = Phase.running
            return Next.again
        }
        // The run waited for: a look at the status.
        const status = card.reg(DC_I2C_SW_STATUS)
        self.*.status = status
        if status & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS == DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_SW {
            self.*.looks += 1
            const late = if paced { self.*.looks >= self.*.bound } else { card.time() >= self.*.started + self.*.bound * 1000 }
            if late { return self.letGo(card, hands, Failure.stays_busy) }
            self.*.pause = 1
            return Next.wait
        }
        const failure = judged(status)
        const final = &self.*.messages[self.*.count - 1]
        if failure == Failure.none && final.*.read {
            // The bytes received lie behind those that were sent.
            self.put(hands, DC_I2C_DATA, DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_INDEX_WRITE | DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA_RW | (self.*.used << shiftOf(DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_INDEX)))
            while self.*.got < final.*.count {
                self.*.received[self.*.got] = @intCast(u8, fieldOf(card.reg(DC_I2C_DATA), DC_I2C_DATA__DC_I2C_DATA))
                self.*.got += 1
            }
        }
        return self.letGo(card, hands, failure)
    }

    // The engine let go (release_engine), then every word that was found
    // put back where it holds something else.
    fn letGo(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, failure: Failure) -> Next {
        const mask = DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK + 4 * self.*.line
        const speed = DC_I2C_DDC1_SPEED + 2 * self.*.line
        const setup = DC_I2C_DDC1_SETUP + 2 * self.*.line
        const stuck = card.reg(DC_I2C_SW_STATUS) & DC_I2C_SW_STATUS__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS == DC_I2C_STATUS__DC_I2C_STATUS_USED_BY_SW
        self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, 0, if stuck { DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SOFT_RESET | DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET } else { DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_SW_STATUS_RESET })
        self.update(card, hands, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_SW_USE_I2C_REG_REQ, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_SW_DONE_USING_I2C_REG)
        var k: usize = 0
        while k < self.*.count {
            self.back(card, hands, DC_I2C_TRANSACTION0 + k, self.*.kept.transaction[k], 0)
            k += 1
        }
        self.back(card, hands, speed, self.*.kept.speed, 0)
        self.back(card, hands, setup, self.*.kept.setup, 0)
        // (Never the bit that starts a run; the arbitration word's status
        // field is the card's to say.)
        self.back(card, hands, DC_I2C_CONTROL, self.*.kept.control & ~DC_I2C_CONTROL__DC_I2C_GO, 0)
        self.back(card, hands, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION, self.*.kept.arbitration, DC_I2C_ARBITRATION__DC_I2C_REG_RW_CNTL_STATUS)
        self.back(card, hands, mask, self.*.kept.mask, 0)
        return self.ended(failure)
    }

    // The word `number` given what it held (`value`) when it holds
    // something else, the bits of `apart` not counted; then looked at
    // once more.
    fn back(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, number: usize, value: usize, apart: usize) -> void {
        if card.reg(number) & ~apart == value & ~apart { return }
        self.put(hands, number, value)
        if card.reg(number) & ~apart != value & ~apart && self.*.restored {
            self.*.restored = false
            self.*.stuck = number
        }
    }
}

// What became of reading an EDID.
pub const Outcome = enum(u8) {
    // Nobody was asked.
    not_asked,
    // Nothing acknowledged the EDID's address: nothing is there that has
    // one, or it is off.
    no_answer,
    // The engine could not be used, or a transfer failed every time
    // (`failure` says how).
    failed,
    // What was read as the base block does not hold (`sum`: what its
    // bytes add up to, 0 when it is the header that is wrong).
    does_not_hold,
    // `count` bytes in `blocks` blocks.
    read
}

// An EDID as read from a connector.
pub const Heard = struct {
    outcome: Outcome
    failure: Failure
    line: usize
    bytes: [ROOM]u8
    count: usize
    blocks: usize
    // How many extension blocks the base block names; whether all of
    // them, up to what is kept here, were read and hold.
    extensions: usize
    complete: bool
    sum: usize
    // The status word of the transfer that failed last, how many
    // transfers were made and how many writes, how long it took
    // (nanoseconds), and whether every word written was put back (`stuck`:
    // the first that was not).
    status: usize
    transfers: usize
    writes: usize
    took: usize
    restored: bool
    stuck: usize

    pub fn clear(self: *Self) -> void {
        self.*.outcome = Outcome.not_asked
        self.*.failure = Failure.none
        self.*.line = NONE
        self.*.count = 0
        self.*.blocks = 0
        self.*.extensions = 0
        self.*.complete = false
        self.*.sum = 0
        self.*.status = 0
        self.*.transfers = 0
        self.*.writes = 0
        self.*.took = 0
        self.*.restored = true
        self.*.stuck = NONE
    }

    pub fn data(self: *const Self) -> []const u8 { return self.*.bytes[0..self.*.count] }
}

// What `count` bytes add up to in eight bits.
fn sumOf(bytes: []const u8, at: usize, count: usize) -> usize {
    var sum: usize = 0
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < count {
        sum += @intCast(usize, bytes[at + k])
        k += 1
    }
    return sum & 0xFF
}

fn headed(bytes: []const u8) -> bool {
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < 8 {
        const wanted: u8 = if k == 0 || k == 7 { 0 } else { 0xFF }
        if bytes[k] != wanted { return false }
        k += 1
    }
    return true
}

const Stage = enum(u8) { idle, probe, block, over }

pub const Reader = struct {
    stage: Stage
    transfer: Transfer
    heard: Heard
    lines: usize
    crystal: usize
    // The block being read, how many are to be read, how often its
    // transfer was made and how often the block was read; when the
    // reading began.
    block: usize
    wanted: usize
    tries: usize
    reads: usize
    began: usize
    pause: usize

    pub fn idle(self: *Self) -> void {
        self.*.stage = Stage.idle
        self.*.heard.clear()
    }

    pub fn busy(self: *const Self) -> bool { return self.*.stage == Stage.probe || self.*.stage == Stage.block }

    // The reading of the EDID on `wire` begun (nothing is touched before
    // the first `step`). `lines`: how many DDC lines the card has;
    // `crystal`: the ROM's reference clock in kHz (0: not known).
    pub fn start(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, wire: *const Wire, lines: usize, crystal: usize) -> void {
        self.*.heard.clear()
        self.*.heard.line = wire.*.line
        self.*.lines = lines
        self.*.crystal = crystal
        self.*.block = 0
        self.*.wanted = 1
        self.*.tries = 0
        self.*.reads = 0
        self.*.pause = 0
        self.*.began = card.time()
        self.*.stage = Stage.probe
        if wire.*.line == NONE {
            self.give(card, Outcome.failed, Failure.no_line)
        } else if !wire.*.hardware {
            self.give(card, Outcome.failed, Failure.not_hardware)
        } else {
            self.ask()
        }
    }

    // The transfer for where the reading stands.
    fn ask(self: *Self) -> void {
        const t = &self.*.transfer
        t.begin(self.*.heard.line, self.*.lines, self.*.crystal)
        if self.*.stage == Stage.probe {
            t.write(DDC_ADDR, 0)
            t.read(DDC_ADDR, 1)
        } else {
            if self.*.block >= 2 { t.write(DDC_SEGMENT_ADDR, self.*.block >> 1) }
            t.write(DDC_ADDR, (self.*.block * EDID_LENGTH) & 0xFF)
            t.read(DDC_ADDR, EDID_LENGTH)
        }
        self.*.tries += 1
    }

    fn give(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, outcome: Outcome, failure: Failure) -> void {
        self.*.heard.outcome = outcome
        self.*.heard.failure = failure
        self.*.heard.took = card.time() - self.*.began
        self.*.stage = Stage.over
    }

    // What is kept so far is the EDID: whole blocks that hold.
    fn kept(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card) -> void {
        self.*.heard.count = self.*.heard.blocks * EDID_LENGTH
        self.*.heard.complete = self.*.heard.blocks == self.*.wanted && self.*.heard.extensions + 1 == self.*.wanted
        self.give(card, Outcome.read, Failure.none)
    }

    // The next piece of the reading; see `Transfer.step`.
    pub fn step(self: *Self, card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, paced: bool) -> Next {
        if !self.busy() { return Next.done }
        const t = &self.*.transfer
        const next = t.step(card, hands, paced)
        if next != Next.done {
            self.*.pause = t.*.pause
            return next
        }
        const h = &self.*.heard
        h.*.transfers += 1
        h.*.writes += t.*.writes
        if !t.*.restored && h.*.restored {
            h.*.restored = false
            h.*.stuck = t.*.stuck
        }
        if !t.succeeded() {
            h.*.status = t.*.status
            // What no further try can change.
            const refused = t.*.failure == Failure.no_line || t.*.failure == Failure.no_such_line || t.*.failure == Failure.no_answer_from_card || t.*.failure == Failure.no_clock
            if !refused && self.*.tries < TRANSFER_TRIES && card.time() - self.*.began < BUDGET {
                self.ask()
                return Next.again
            }
            if self.*.stage == Stage.block && self.*.block != 0 {
                // (An extension block that cannot be read: the blocks
                // before it are the EDID.)
                self.kept(card)
            } else if self.*.stage == Stage.probe && t.*.failure == Failure.not_acknowledged {
                self.give(card, Outcome.no_answer, t.*.failure)
            } else {
                self.give(card, Outcome.failed, t.*.failure)
            }
            return Next.done
        }
        if self.*.stage == Stage.probe {
            self.*.stage = Stage.block
            self.*.tries = 0
            self.ask()
            return Next.again
        }
        // A block: does it hold?
        const at = self.*.block * EDID_LENGTH
        var k: usize = 0
        var zero = true
        while k < EDID_LENGTH {
            h.*.bytes[at + k] = t.*.received[k]
            if t.*.received[k] != 0 { zero = false }
            k += 1
        }
        self.*.reads += 1
        const sum = sumOf(h.*.bytes[0..], at, EDID_LENGTH)
        const holds = sum == 0 && (self.*.block != 0 || headed(h.*.bytes[0..]))
        if !holds {
            // (A base block of nothing but zeroes is not read again:
            // edid_block_read's early end.)
            const again = self.*.reads < BLOCK_READS && !(self.*.block == 0 && zero) && card.time() - self.*.began < BUDGET
            if again {
                self.*.tries = 0
                self.ask()
                return Next.again
            }
            if self.*.block == 0 {
                h.*.sum = sum
                self.give(card, Outcome.does_not_hold, Failure.none)
            } else {
                self.kept(card)
            }
            return Next.done
        }
        h.*.blocks = self.*.block + 1
        if self.*.block == 0 {
            h.*.extensions = @intCast(usize, h.*.bytes[126])
            self.*.wanted = if h.*.extensions + 1 > BLOCKS { BLOCKS } else { h.*.extensions + 1 }
        }
        if h.*.blocks == self.*.wanted || card.time() - self.*.began >= BUDGET {
            self.kept(card)
            return Next.done
        }
        self.*.block += 1
        self.*.tries = 0
        self.*.reads = 0
        self.ask()
        return Next.again
    }
}

// The EDID on `wire` read from beginning to end, with Linux's pauses made
// by `pause` (microseconds): for a boot, where nothing else runs.
pub fn read(card: *const scanout.Card, hands: *const Hands, pause: fn(usize) -> void, wire: *const Wire, lines: usize, crystal: usize, reader: *Reader) -> void {
    reader.start(card, wire, lines, crystal)
    while true {
        const next = reader.step(card, hands, true)
        if next == Next.done { break }
        if next == Next.wait { pause(reader.*.pause) }
    }
}
